SOLUTIONS={
1:('DIG-01','Subtract 10 from 201 to get 191, then add one back because only 9 was to be subtracted. The result is 192.'),
2:('DATA-03','Compare the column heights. The tallest belongs to the highest scorer; the next tallest is Evan’s. Thus Evan scored the second-highest number of goals.'),
3:('DIG-01','Separate the number into place-value groups: 6 million + 203 thousand + 6. Written with three-digit groups, this is 6 203 006.'),
4:('LOGIC-02','A card covered by another must have been dropped earlier. Follow the overlaps from bottom to top: the ace is under 4, which is under 5, then 3, then the topmost 2. The dropping order is A,4,5,3,2.'),
5:('SPATIAL-01','Moving from 1st Street to 4th Street crosses three blocks, not four. Moving from 1st Avenue to 3rd Avenue crosses two. Using the compass shown, these movements are 3 blocks east and 2 blocks north.'),
6:('DATA-01','There are three hearts (A,J,9), no diamonds, two spades, two picture cards and one even-numbered card. Hearts are the most numerous of the offered categories, so a heart is most likely.'),
7:('SOLID-03','A triangular prism has two triangular ends and three rectangular sides: five faces. Each triangular end contributes three edges, and three further edges join the ends: 3 + 3 + 3 = 9. This is shape D.'),
8:('GEO-05','The whole journey is 230 km, so halfway is 115 km. Blanchetown is 106 km away, only 9 km from halfway. The other listed towns are farther from 115, so Blanchetown is the best stop.'),
9:('DIG-01','Align the decimal places: 25.00 − 8.25 = 16.75. The difference in height is 16.75 m.'),
10:('SEQ-01','There are nine equal intervals from 24 to 42. Each interval is (42 − 24)/9 = 2. P is six intervals after 24, so its value is 24 + 6 × 2 = 36.'),
11:('COUNT-01','The smallest sum is 0 + 0 = 0 and the largest is 9 + 9 = 18. Every integer in between can occur. Counting inclusively gives 18 − 0 + 1 = 19 different sums. This counts distinct totals, not the 100 ordered dice outcomes.'),
12:('LOGIC-02','In the top row, the middle entry cannot be 1 (already in its row) or 2 (already in its column), so it is 3. The top-right entry is therefore 2. The right column must then contain 1 and 3 in its two remaining cells N and M, so N + M = 4.'),
13:('RATE-01','Lovelace lived from December 1815 to November 1852, about 37 years. Babbage lived from December 1791 to October 1871, about 80 years. The difference, to the nearest year, is about 80 − 37 = 43 years.'),
14:('FRAC-01','Divide the available ribbon by the amount per decoration: 12 ÷ (2/5) = 12 × 5/2 = 30. All 12 metres are used by 30 decorations.'),
15:('RATE-02','Bernadette clears 1/60 of the yard per minute and Andrew 1/30. Their combined rate is 1/60 + 2/60 = 3/60 = 1/20. They finish one whole yard in 20 minutes.'),
16:('SOLID-01','A tile side is 50 cm = 0.5 m. There are 6/0.5 = 12 tiles along the room and 4/0.5 = 8 across. Thus 12 × 8 = 96 tiles are needed. Alternatively divide 24 m² by 0.25 m² per tile.'),
17:('LOGIC-02','The only allowed neighbouring pairs are 1–3, 1–4 and 2–4. To link all four numbers in a row, these three pairs must form the chain 3–1–4–2. Reversing gives 2–4–1–3. These are the only two valid orders.'),
18:('ALG-03','Holly’s card is 21. Mike sees Holly and John and says 36, so John’s card is 36 − 21 = 15. Chris sees John and Anne and says 33, so Anne’s card is 33 − 15 = 18. Notice that the spoken totals are not the speakers’ own card numbers.'),
19:('GEO-02','Split the regular star into twelve equal small equilateral triangles. One original triangle contains nine of them and has area 9 cm², so each small triangle has area 1 cm². The star’s area is therefore 12 cm². Equivalently, the overlap is 6 cm² and the union is 9 + 9 − 6 = 12.'),
20:('FRAC-01','Being awake for three-quarters of the journey means sleeping for one-quarter. That quarter lasted 3/4 hour. The full journey lasted 4 × 3/4 = 3 hours.'),
21:('ALG-03','Changing the input from 2 to 15 increases it by 13, while the output increases from 11 to 50, a change of 39. The multiplier is 39/13 = 3. The added number is 11 − 2 × 3 = 5. For input 6 the result is 6 × 3 + 5 = 23.'),
22:('GEO-04','Unfold the map by reflecting the stained part across each crease. The diagonal waterline reverses slope in neighbouring panels, and the lower half is the reflected continuation of the upper half. E is the pattern whose stain boundaries meet correctly across all folds. A repeated diagonal in the same direction would not result from unfolding.'),
23:('SEQ-01','A regular octahedron has 12 edges. The first 3 rods already form its bottom triangular face, so completing it adds 9 rods. Every later octahedron shares its bottom triangular face with the one below and also adds 9 rods. Thus 3 + 9n = 2019, giving n = 2016/9 = 224 octahedra.'),
24:('SOLID-03','Use the three views to determine the opposite pairs: A opposite M, C opposite E, and D opposite F. From behind, the row reverses left-to-right, so the back letters must read E,A,M. Track the orientation of the printed faces as well: when the front C is upright, its opposite E is a quarter-turn sideways, while the back A and M are upright. Only D has both the correct order and the correct letter orientations.'),
25:('COUNT-06','List unordered score triples adding to 11: (7,3,1), (5,5,1), and (5,3,3). There are 6 orders of the first triple, and 3 orders of each repeated-number triple, according to which named race receives each score. Total: 6 + 3 + 3 = 12 ways. No triple involving 0 can add to 11 using the remaining available scores.')}
